1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:07,084
Welcome to Dot Goose, part two. Six backend examples now share
the same web application builder pattern.

2
00:00:07,364 --> 00:00:16,084
Each Goose program has program main, called once by main. We
will compare its behavior, executable size, CPU time, and peak

3
00:00:16,084 --> 00:00:16,580
memory.

4
00:00:17,342 --> 00:00:18,302
First example.

5
00:00:19,102 --> 00:00:20,104
Health endpoint.

6
00:00:20,654 --> 00:00:27,854
Explanation: Create slim builder receives the arguments. Clear
providers configures logging, and Build creates the

7
00:00:27,854 --> 00:00:28,612
application.

8
00:00:28,892 --> 00:00:36,596
Map Get registers the health handler. Run starts the server.
The Goose function named program main contains all five

9
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:39,452
startup statements, and main calls it once.

10
00:00:40,217 --> 00:00:47,790
Explanation: Results dot Text returns the raw JSON as text
with an application slash JSON content type.

11
00:00:48,070 --> 00:00:55,723
Triple quotes work in both languages, so the quotation marks
around status and ok need no backslash escapes. Repeated

12
00:00:55,723 --> 00:00:57,947
requests use the compiled handler.

13
00:00:58,717 --> 00:00:59,719
Second example.

14
00:01:00,519 --> 00:01:01,565
Routing and query.

15
00:01:02,115 --> 00:01:09,889
Explanation: The same builder pattern sets up the application.
The endpoint uses an integer route constraint and checks the

16
00:01:09,889 --> 00:01:10,584
query text.

17
00:01:10,864 --> 00:01:18,180
Dot Goose stores the route and a compiled handler ID during
startup. It does not repeat the builder calls for each

18
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:18,693
request.

19
00:01:19,425 --> 00:01:26,636
Explanation: A valid ID reaches the handler. The include
details query selects one of two JSON bodies.

20
00:01:26,916 --> 00:01:34,041
The same tests also check a missing query, invalid text, a
nonnumeric ID, and an out of range integer.

21
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:35,717
Third example.

22
00:01:36,517 --> 00:01:37,456
Ordered middleware.

23
00:01:38,006 --> 00:01:41,995
Explanation: Both programs register middleware before the
endpoint.

24
00:01:42,275 --> 00:01:50,489
The first stage validates the request ID. The second adds the
endpoint stage. A rejected request stops before the handler.

25
00:01:51,258 --> 00:02:00,389
Explanation: The request ID check runs before the endpoint. If
it fails, the middleware sends an HTTP four hundred response.

26
00:02:00,669 --> 00:02:07,560
A valid request receives the echoed ID and the ordered stages
header. The route handler then returns pong.

27
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:09,217
Fourth example.

28
00:02:10,017 --> 00:02:11,660
JSON body validation.

29
00:02:12,210 --> 00:02:18,354
Explanation: Both applications register a post endpoint and
require a JSON content type.

30
00:02:18,634 --> 00:02:25,973
The handler parses the body, validates name and quantity, and
returns a created response with the same location header.

31
00:02:26,717 --> 00:02:31,773
Explanation: The JSON reader and field checks live inside the
registered handler.

32
00:02:32,053 --> 00:02:41,850
Malformed JSON, a wrong content type, or invalid fields return
an HTTP four hundred response. Valid input returns two hundred

33
00:02:41,850 --> 00:02:46,474
one, with the same JSON and location header as ASP dot net.

34
00:02:47,217 --> 00:02:48,134
Fifth example.

35
00:02:48,934 --> 00:02:50,193
Request-scoped state.

36
00:02:50,743 --> 00:02:54,903
Explanation: The builder registers a scoped Visit service
before Build.

37
00:02:55,183 --> 00:03:02,372
The middleware and endpoint get the same Visit instance during
one request. The next request gets a fresh instance.

38
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:08,573
Explanation: Get required service returns the request scoped
Visit value by reference.

39
00:03:08,853 --> 00:03:16,590
The middleware advances it once and the endpoint advances it
again. Each response reports step two, including after an

40
00:03:16,590 --> 00:03:17,771
unrelated request.

41
00:03:18,508 --> 00:03:19,468
Sixth example.

42
00:03:20,268 --> 00:03:21,890
MVC controller and view.

43
00:03:22,440 --> 00:03:29,530
Explanation: Create builder enables the full hosting setup,
and Add controllers with views enables controller

44
00:03:29,530 --> 00:03:30,376
registration.

45
00:03:30,656 --> 00:03:38,968
Map controllers registers the catalog endpoint. The Razor file
is compiled into a Goose render function and reused for each

46
00:03:38,968 --> 00:03:39,509
request.

47
00:03:40,258 --> 00:03:45,336
Explanation: The catalog controller checks the ID and builds
the model for its view.

48
00:03:45,616 --> 00:03:53,468
The Goose mapping uses the same model fields. It calls render
Show, which was generated from the original Razor file before

49
00:03:53,468 --> 00:03:54,234
compilation.

50
00:03:54,967 --> 00:04:00,812
The Razor compiler reads the template at build time and
creates an ordinary Goose render function.

51
00:04:01,092 --> 00:04:09,329
It supports the model directive, model properties, and a
doubled at sign for literal text. Model text is HTML escaped.

52
00:04:09,329 --> 00:04:12,121
The server reuses the compiled function.

53
00:04:12,883 --> 00:04:17,641
These are the executable sizes of the six updated builder
based examples.

54
00:04:17,921 --> 00:04:25,939
The smaller Goose build uses the system C library. Static
Goose includes libc. Dot net Native A O T is shown where

55
00:04:25,939 --> 00:04:30,230
supported, and MVC uses the self-contained single-file build.

56
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:37,751
The application statements use the same number of lines as C
sharp, or fewer, across all six examples.

57
00:04:38,031 --> 00:04:46,555
Goose also has four explicit entry point lines. This table
shows those separately and includes the complete source total.

58
00:04:46,555 --> 00:04:50,148
MVC includes the same Razor template on both sides.

59
00:04:50,883 --> 00:04:59,976
Server CPU time includes startup, two thousand requests, and
shutdown. Memory is peak resident memory. The dot net column

60
00:04:59,976 --> 00:05:04,259
uses Native A O T except for MVC, which uses single-file.

61
00:05:04,539 --> 00:05:09,275
These results describe this sequential loopback workload and
this Linux machine.
